+      "chars": 1812,
+      "file": "corpus/08-hareet-ai.md",
+      "golden": null,
+      "heading": "How a change is made",
+      "id": "08-hareet-ai#9.1",
+      "kind": "section",
+      "text": "- `[P]` Every change to hareet.ai starts as a sentence and ends with a person's merge. — https://github.com/hareet/h4reet-ai/blob/main/.specify/memory/constitution.md\n- `[P]` First, the intent: what's wanted, and why, in the words of whoever wants it. Hareet accepts it or closes it, and the survival rate is published. — https://github.com/hareet/h4reet-ai/blob/main/intent/README.md\n- `[P]` A spec-writer agent turns it into a spec, with acceptance criteria a test can check; its questions go to Hareet, and his answers are kept word for word. — https://github.com/hareet/h4reet-ai/blob/main/.claude/agents/spec-writer.md\n- `[P]` Then the plan: the files, the order, the risks, the cost, and what happens when it degrades. It's approved before any code exists. — https://github.com/hareet/h4reet-ai/blob/main/docs/how-we-build/lifecycle.md\n- `[P]` Then the tasks, and a read-only check that the spec, the plan, and the tasks agree. — https://github.com/hareet/h4reet-ai/blob/main/docs/how-we-build/lifecycle.md\n- `[P]` An implementer agent writes it on a feature branch, task by task, with tests, and every new feature brings its own telemetry, a dashboard panel, a cost estimate, its degraded behavior, and a disclosure row if a byte leaves a visitor's device. — https://github.com/hareet/h4reet-ai/blob/main/.claude/agents/implementer.md\n- `[P]` A reviewer agent, read-only and never the one that wrote the change, checks it for bugs, security, compliance, and cost. Its findings inform the merge; they never replace it. — https://github.com/hareet/h4reet-ai/blob/main/REVIEW.md\n- `[P]` Changing a feature works the same way: a newer spec names each clause it amends, and when the code departs from the plan, the plan is corrected in the same commit. — https://github.com/hareet/h4reet-ai/blob/main/CLAUDE.md"
+    },
